+## 2026-03-26: PSF Steering, Oracle Detour, and What AM275x Actually Changed in Our Thinking
+
+### What We Thought
+**This is a prime opportunity to explain packet flow in the thesis** 
+Observed: the earliest hard problem was not "we need a better timestamp source"   <!-- somewhat redundant just keep in mind that the overall goal is PHY PSF utilization, also it doesnt really mention the reason for why we split of PSFs in the first place. maybe gptp_starvation_fix_normalized.patch gives an indication. please clarify here.--> 
+but "PHY status frames are arriving in the wrong part of the receive topology and
+can destabilize gPTP if they are treated like ordinary PTP traffic." The first  <!-- specify what "the wrong part" is, this being somewhat on the path to PTP, please specify where exactly -->
+serious response to that was [`psf_ingest_fix_clean.patch`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/psf_ingest_fix_clean.patch),
+which is best understood as a steering/containment patch, not as a worker/drain
+architecture. That patch added `rxFilterDstMac` in
+[`cb_ethernet.h`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_combase/cb_ethernet.h),
+propagated it into the LLD config path in
+[`cb_lld_ethernet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_combase/tilld/cb_lld_ethernet.c),
+used it from
+[`lld_gptpnet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_gptp/tilld/lld_gptpnet.c)
+to narrow the owner-side RX path to `ETH_P_1588 + gPTP multicast MAC`, and
+changed
+[`lldenet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_combase/tilld/sitara/lldenet.c)
+to support delete-before-replace classifier management via
+`CPSW_ALE_IOCTL_GET_POLICER`, plus explicit owner-side `owner-drop-psf` behavior.
+Inference: the intent was sound for the old crash mode. If PSFs cannot reach the
+gPTP-owned receive path, they also cannot reach the parser/state-machine path.
+But that was still fundamentally a steering fix. It did not create a safe place
+for PSFs to exist; it only tried to keep them out.
+
+### What Broke in the Next Idea
+
+Observed: the next branch idea, captured in
+[`dp83tc812_timestamp_oracle_unstaged_clean.patch`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/dp83tc812_timestamp_oracle_unstaged_clean.patch),
+went in a different direction. Instead of first redesigning ownership and
+draining, it tried to make the PHY useful as a keyed timestamp oracle by adding
+PHY IOCTLs for `WAIT_TX_TIMESTAMP`, `GET_TX_TIMESTAMP`, and `GET_RX_TIMESTAMP`.
+This was an attempt to make the PHY answer timestamp lookup questions by message
+identity, using the status-frame store as the raw material, without yet doing the
+full receive-path redesign.
+
+Observed and important anti-conflation note: in the current AWR SDK, that oracle
+work does **not** mean PSFs are already feeding the live gPTP state-machine timing
+path. The live AWR Tx timestamp ingress remains CPTS-based in
+[`lld_gptpnet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_gptp/tilld/lld_gptpnet.c),
+which queues `txts_info` and calls `LLDTSyncGetTxTime()`. That function in
+[`sitara/lldtsync.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_combase/tilld/sitara/lldtsync.c)
+still resolves TxTS through `ENET_TIMESYNC_IOCTL_GET_ETH_TX_TIMESTAMP`. The
+result is passed into `GPTPNET_EVENT_TXTS` and then into the state-machine side in
+[`gptpman.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_gptp/gptpman.c).
+Meanwhile the PSF side is separate: `LLDTSyncProcPhyStatusFrame()` forwards frames
+to `ENET_PHY_IOCTL_PTP_PROCESS_STATUS_FRAME`, and
+[`dp83tc812.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/enet/core/src/phy/dp83tc812.c)
+currently stores decoded PSFs into `gDp83tc812PsfRing` and prints debug, but does
+not in the present AWR tree feed those PSFs directly into the gPTP state machine.
+Future readers need to keep this boundary straight: "PHY timestamp oracle work
+exists" is not the same statement as "AWR gPTP timing already runs off PSFs."
+
+### What the Logs Actually Taught Us
+
+Observed: [`test19.txt`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/test19.txt)
+is still the right baseline for the "this mostly forked fine" period. It already
+contains one `missing TxTS` and one `TxTS is not expected` mismatch, but only one
+clear mismatch pair. That matters because it tells us the later flood of warnings
+was not the original steady state; there was probably already a latent issue, but
+not at the later frequency.
+
+Observed: [`test26.txt`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/test26.txt)
+showed the first Milestone 1 worker/drain plumbing was structurally alive but not
+yet owning PSFs. The worker started, woke up, and logged
+`LLDEnetLogStatusDrain: ... drainedStatus=0`, while default flow still printed
+`default-flow summary ... phyStatus=58`. Inference: Milestone 1 proved we could
+build the worker-visible drain path, but the old steering still meant PSFs were
+not arriving where the worker could actually peel them off.
+
+Observed: [`test27.txt`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/test27.txt)
+showed that the first reversal was incomplete. Startup failed with
+`CPSW_ALE_IOCTL_GET_POLICER failed -13`. The reason was not mysterious after
+inspection: we had removed some of the narrowing and drop behavior, but the old
+delete-before-replace classifier stack from
+[`psf_ingest_fix_clean.patch`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/psf_ingest_fix_clean.patch)
+was still active in
+[`lldenet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_combase/tilld/sitara/lldenet.c).
+That was the point where it became obvious that "undo the bad steering" meant
+undoing a whole steering stack, not just one filter assignment.
+
+Observed: [`test28.txt`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/test28.txt)
+is the first log that really demonstrates the redesign working as intended at the
+routing/ownership layer. Startup succeeds, `dev:tilld0 open success` comes back,
+the status worker starts, and `LLDEnetLogStatusDrain` increments
+`drainedStatus=1/2/3`. gPTP reaches `gmsync=true`. That is the missing proof from
+`test26`: PSFs are now in the worker-visible path instead of being owned primarily
+by default flow.
+
+Working hypothesis: the increase in `missing TxTS` / `TxTS is not expected`
+warnings after the worker redesign is best treated as a secondary timing/scheduling
+effect, not as evidence that PSFs are being injected into the gPTP state machine
+as frames. The strongest code-level suspect in the current AWR tree is still the
+CPTS-side polling logic in
+[`lld_gptpnet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_gptp/tilld/lld_gptpnet.c):
+`gptpnet_catch_event()` always runs `process_txts()` after every semaphore wake,
+the new worker now posts that semaphore more often, and `process_txts()` pops a
+pending `txts_info` entry before confirming that `LLDTSyncGetTxTime()` can
+actually resolve a CPTS timestamp. If the CPTS event is not ready yet, the request
+is effectively dropped. This fits both the single mismatch in
+[`test19.txt`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/test19.txt) and the more
+frequent warnings in
+[`test28.txt`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/test28.txt), but it is
+still a hypothesis and should not be written down as proven root cause.
+
+### What AM275x Actually Taught Us
+
+Observed: the architectural inspiration from AM275x was not "copy its timestamp
+backend" but "copy its ownership/drain structure." In the AM275x SDK,
+[`AM275x lld_gptpnet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/freertos_sdk_am275x_11_00_00_16/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_gptp/tilld/lld_gptpnet.c)
+contains a dedicated `statPktSem`, a status-frame processing task, and a call to
+`cb_lld_process_status_frames()` before reposting the main semaphore. On the LLD
+side,
+[`AM275x lldenet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/freertos_sdk_am275x_11_00_00_16/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_combase/tilld/sitara/lldenet.c)
+contains the status-frame drain path that forwards those frames into
+`LLDTSyncProcPhyStatusFrame()`. Inference: AM275x showed us the architectural
+separation we were missing. Status-frame ownership/drain, ordinary gPTP RX, and
+timestamp/status backend plumbing should be distinct concerns.
+
+Observed: this is exactly where FreeRTOS and the common TSN wrapper layer help.
+The right primitives are not clever packet heuristics; they are task boundaries,
+semaphores, and a wake-and-drain model. AM275x showed a practical pattern using a
+dedicated worker, a status semaphore, and a thin wrapper boundary between gPTP and
+lower-level timestamp/status handling. That led directly to the Milestone 1 design
+on AWR.
+
+Anti-conflation note: AM275x is the source of the **worker/drain architecture**,
+not proof that AWR has already adopted the AM275x end-to-end timestamp model. At
+this stage AWR still uses CPTS for the live TxTS ingress into gPTP. PSF handling
+on AWR is being restructured around ownership and draining, but the timestamp
+backend used by the state machine is still the AWR/CPTS one.
+
+### Where Milestone 1 and Milestone 1.5 Fit
+
+Observed: [`Milestone1.patch`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/Milestone1.patch)
+is the real pivot, not a minor patch. It adds the status-frame header query
+support, thin `lldtsync` wrappers for PSF processing, the
+`cb_lld_process_status_frames()` / `LLDEnetProcessStatusFrames()` plumbing, and a
+dedicated status worker plus semaphore wiring in
+[`lld_gptpnet.c`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/mcu_plus_sdk_awr2x44p_10_01_00_04/source/networking/tsn/tsn-stack/tsn_gptp/tilld/lld_gptpnet.c).
+This was the moment we stopped pretending a steering tweak would be enough and
+started giving PSFs a proper staging/drain path.
+
+Observed: [`Milestone1_5.patch`](/mnt/c/ti/mmwave_mcuplus_sdk_04_07_01_04/Milestone1_5.patch)
+was the first corrective reversal on top of that redesign. It stopped default-flow
+ownership, reopened owner-side visibility, and removed explicit `owner-drop-psf`.
+But it was still incomplete because it did not yet remove the entire old
+delete-before-replace ALE steering stack, which is why `test27` still failed.
+
+What we learned: the original branch assumed we could make PSFs safe mostly by
+changing steering rules. The later work showed that this was the wrong level of
+abstraction. We actually needed an ownership/scheduling architecture, and the
+FreeRTOS-style toolset already available to us, tasks plus semaphores plus a clean
+drain hook, was the right way to build it. The remaining timing warnings matter,
+but they sit **after** this redesign lesson, not before it. The next mistake would
+be to forget that chronology and start treating the current AWR tree as if it were
+already an AM275x clone or as if PSF parser work were already the live source of
+gPTP Tx timestamps.
+
